\section{Introduction}

\label{sec:introduction}

Main memory is one of the most critical resources an operating system has to manage. Under contiguous allocation, every process must be placed in a single block of consecutive addresses, so the operating system needs to keep track of which memory slots are free and decide which one to use each time a process is created. This decision, taken by the free memory slot manager, determines how efficiently memory is used: a poor choice leaves small, scattered holes that cannot host new processes, a problem known as \textit{external fragmentation}.

This report documents the first deliverable of the \textit{UR-OS -- Main Memory Management} project for the Operating Systems course at Universidad del Rosario. The project extends the UR-OS simulator, written in Java and originally created by Professor Pedro Wightman, so that students can study the main functionalities of memory management by implementing them directly in the simulator. This first deliverable focuses on \textbf{free memory management (slot management)} under contiguous allocation. Memory allocation and mapping techniques, namely paging and segmentation, are left for the second deliverable.

The objectives of this deliverable are the following:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Implement the \texttt{FirstFit}, \texttt{BestFit} and \texttt{WorstFit} policies as \texttt{FreeMemorySlotManager}s inside the UR-OS simulator.
    \item Design our own scenario in which several free slots are available when a process is created, so that the three policies make different decisions and their behavior can be told apart.
    \item Solve the scenario manually, step by step, and compare those results with the output of the simulator, using screenshots as evidence. This includes checking that each logical address of a \texttt{STORE} operation maps to the physical address expected from the slot chosen by each policy.
    \item Draw conclusions about the behavior of each policy and about the quality of the implementation.
\end{itemize}

The rest of the report follows the structure requested for the deliverable. First, we present the source code of the implemented algorithms together with a description of how each one works. Then, we define the scenario and present the manual solution for each policy. Next, we compare these manual results with the simulator results. Finally, we summarize our conclusions.

