\subsection{Memory management model in UR-OS}

Memory management in UR-OS is organized in three cooperating layers. Understanding them is necessary to see exactly where the new strategies act:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{System Memory Manager} (\code{SystemMemoryManager}): translates logical addresses into physical addresses. For contiguous allocation (\code{SMM\_Contiguous}) the translation is the classical base/limit scheme: $\text{physical} = \text{base} + \text{logical}$, and the access is rejected if $\text{logical} \geq \text{limit}$.
  \item \textbf{Process Memory Manager} (\code{ProcessMemoryManager}): stores, for each process, where it lives in memory. For contiguous allocation (\code{PMM\_Contiguous}) this is a \code{MemorySlot} (base and size) in main memory and another one in swap memory.
  \item \textbf{Free Memory Manager} (\code{FreeMemoryManager}): keeps track of the free space. For contiguous allocation (and segmentation) it is a \code{FreeMemorySlotManager}, which stores a \code{LinkedList<MemorySlot>} of free holes ordered by base address. When a process is created, \code{OS.create\_process()} requests a hole through \code{getSlot(size)}; when a process finishes, \code{reclaimMemory()} returns its hole to the list and \code{fuseSlots()} merges adjacent holes.
\end{enumerate}

The abstract method \code{getSlot(int size)} is the only point where the three strategies differ; this is the method implemented in this deliverable for Best Fit and Worst Fit. The simulated main memory has $1\,048\,576$ bytes (1 MB) and the swap memory is managed by an independent free memory manager of the same type.

