\section{Best Fit}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Design description}

\code{BestFitMemorySlotManager} extends \code{FreeMemorySlotManager} and implements \code{getSlot(int size)}. Rules of the implemented policy:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Full scan}: the whole list of free holes is traversed; unlike First Fit, the search cannot stop at the first valid hole, because a smaller valid hole may appear later in the list.
  \item \textbf{Selection}: among the holes that satisfy \code{canContain(size)} (i.e., $\text{hole size} \geq \text{request}$), the one with the \emph{smallest} size is kept. In case of a tie, the first one found (lowest address) is kept, because the comparison is strict (\code{<}).
  \item \textbf{Exact fit}: if the selected hole has exactly the requested size, it is removed from the free list and returned as the allocated slot.
  \item \textbf{Split}: if the selected hole is larger, \code{assignMemory(size)} cuts the requested amount from the \emph{beginning} of the hole: it returns a new slot $[\text{base}, \text{base}+\text{size})$ and the hole that remains in the list moves its base forward and shrinks.
  \item \textbf{Failure}: if no hole is large enough, an error message is printed and \code{null} is returned.
\end{enumerate}

The rationale of Best Fit is to minimize the leftover produced by each allocation, preserving the large holes for large future requests.

\subsection{Implementation code}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={BestFitMemorySlotManager.java -- Best Fit strategy}]
public class BestFitMemorySlotManager extends FreeMemorySlotManager{

    public BestFitMemorySlotManager(int memSize){
        super(memSize);
    }

    @Override
    public MemorySlot getSlot(int size) {
        MemorySlot best = null;

        //Recorremos toda la lista buscando el slot más pequeño que aún alcance
        for (MemorySlot memorySlot : list) {
            if(memorySlot.canContain(size)){
                if(best == null || memorySlot.getSize() < best.getSize()){
                    best = memorySlot;
                }
            }
        }

        if(best == null){
            //Si ningún slot alcanza, se retorna null
            System.out.println("Error - La memoria solicitada es demasiado grande para la memoria disponible");
            return null;
            }

        if(best.getSize() == size){
            /*Si el tamaño pedido coincide exactamente con el del slot,
              se remueve el slot completo de la lista y se retorna tal cual*/
            list.remove(best);
            return best;
        }else{
            /*Si el slot es más grande de lo pedido, se parte: assignMemory
              actualiza el slot original (que sigue en la lista) y retorna
              un nuevo MemorySlot con el pedazo asignado*/
            return best.assignMemory(size);
        }
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}

The strategy is wired into the operating system in the constructor of \code{OS.java}, which instantiates the free memory manager for main memory (\code{fmm}) and for swap memory (\code{fvmm}) according to the constant \code{MSM}:

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={OS.java -- Selection of the free memory strategy}]
public static final FreeMemorySlotManagerType MSM = FreeMemorySlotManagerType.FIRST_FIT;
...
switch(MSM){
    case FIRST_FIT:
        fmm = new FirstFitMemorySlotManager(SystemOS.MEMORY_SIZE); //Memory
        fvmm= new FirstFitMemorySlotManager(SystemOS.SWAP_MEMORY_SIZE); //Swap memory
        break;
    case BEST_FIT:
        fmm = new BestFitMemorySlotManager(SystemOS.MEMORY_SIZE);
        fvmm = new BestFitMemorySlotManager(SystemOS.SWAP_MEMORY_SIZE);
        break;
    case WORST_FIT:
        fmm = new WorstFitMemorySlotManager(SystemOS.MEMORY_SIZE);
        fvmm = new WorstFitMemorySlotManager(SystemOS.SWAP_MEMORY_SIZE);
        break;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The enumeration \code{FreeMemorySlotManagerType} was extended with the values \code{BEST\_FIT} and \code{WORST\_FIT}.

\subsection{Step-by-step description of the code}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Constructor}: delegates to \code{FreeMemorySlotManager(memSize)}, which initializes the free list with a single hole $[0, \text{memSize})$ covering the whole memory.
  \item \textbf{Search loop}: every hole is tested with \code{canContain(size)}. The variable \code{best} keeps the smallest valid hole found so far; it is replaced only when a strictly smaller valid hole appears.
  \item \textbf{No valid hole}: if \code{best} is still \code{null} after the loop, there is no hole large enough and the method returns \code{null}. Note that this can happen even if the \emph{total} free memory is larger than the request: this is external fragmentation.
  \item \textbf{Exact fit}: the hole object itself is removed from the list and handed to the process.
  \item \textbf{Split}: \code{assignMemory(size)} creates the allocated slot at the old base and updates the hole in place (\code{base += size}, \code{size -= size}), so the list keeps its address ordering without re-sorting.
  \item \textbf{Complexity}: $O(n)$ in the number of free holes, always (the full list is scanned), whereas First Fit is $O(n)$ in the worst case but can stop early.
\end{itemize}

