\subsection{Manual verification}

The manual verification was carried out by tracing the free-hole list by hand, without looking at the program output. For each request the following procedure was applied:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item List the \emph{candidate holes}: the free holes whose size is greater than or equal to the request.
  \item Apply the rule of the strategy to choose one candidate (for Best Fit, the smallest one).
  \item Split the chosen hole: the allocated slot starts at the base of the hole, and the leftover hole starts at $\text{base} + \text{request}$ with size $\text{hole size} - \text{request}$. If the leftover is 0, the hole disappears from the list.
  \item If there are no candidates, the request fails, even if the sum of the free holes is larger than the request.
\end{enumerate}

As a worked example, consider the first request of Scenario A (90 bytes) under Best Fit. The three holes H1 (300), H2 (100) and H3 (400) are all candidates; the smallest is H2, so the slot $[400, 490)$ is allocated and H2 becomes $[490, 500)$ with $100 - 90 = 10$ bytes. The remaining requests are traced in the same way in the following tables.

