\section{Worst Fit}
% ============================================================

\subsection{Design description}

\code{WorstFitMemorySlotManager} also extends \code{FreeMemorySlotManager}; its structure is symmetric to Best Fit, but the selection criterion is reversed. Rules of the implemented policy:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Full scan}: the whole list of free holes is traversed.
  \item \textbf{Selection}: among the holes that satisfy \code{canContain(size)}, the one with the \emph{largest} size is kept (ties are resolved in favor of the lowest address, because the comparison is strict, \code{>}).
  \item \textbf{Exact fit / split / failure}: identical to Best Fit.
\end{enumerate}

The rationale of Worst Fit is the opposite of Best Fit: by always cutting from the largest hole, the leftover is as large as possible, and therefore more likely to be useful for a future request.

\subsection{Implementation code}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={WorstFitMemorySlotManager.java -- Worst Fit strategy}]
public class WorstFitMemorySlotManager extends FreeMemorySlotManager{

    public WorstFitMemorySlotManager(int memSize){
        super(memSize);
    }

    @Override
    public MemorySlot getSlot(int size) {
        MemorySlot worst = null;

        //Recorremos toda la lista buscando el slot más grande que alcance
        for (MemorySlot memorySlot : list) {
            if(memorySlot.canContain(size)){
                if(worst == null || memorySlot.getSize() > worst.getSize()){
                    worst = memorySlot;
                }
            }
        }

        if(worst == null){
            System.out.println("Error: La memoria solicitada es demasiado grande para la memoria disponible");
            return null;
        }

        if(worst.getSize() == size){
            list.remove(worst);
            return worst;
        }else{
            return worst.assignMemory(size);
        }
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Step-by-step description of the code}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Search loop}: the variable \code{worst} keeps the largest valid hole found so far. Since the largest hole of the list is valid whenever \emph{any} hole is valid, the chosen hole is simply the largest hole in memory.
  \item \textbf{Exact fit and split}: identical to Best Fit, reusing \code{list.remove()} and \code{assignMemory()} from the base classes.
  \item \textbf{Complexity}: $O(n)$ in the number of free holes, always.
\end{itemize}

