``Use of free memory'' is the ratio between allocated bytes and the 800 bytes that were free at the beginning.

The two scenarios were designed so that each new strategy wins in one of them, which shows that no strategy is universally better:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Best Fit} wins when requests of very different sizes arrive and a large hole must be preserved for a later large request (Scenario A). Its weakness is the generation of tiny, useless fragments (10 bytes in Scenario A).
  \item \textbf{Worst Fit} wins when requests are of similar, medium size, because the leftover of the largest hole remains large enough to be reused (Scenario B). Its weakness is that it quickly destroys large holes, so a later large request can fail (Scenario A).
  \item \textbf{First Fit} behaves in between: in Scenario A it matches Worst Fit and in Scenario B it matches Best Fit. Its advantage is not the quality of the placement but the speed of the search, since it can stop at the first valid hole instead of scanning the whole list.
\end{itemize}

In all cases where a request failed, the total free memory was larger than the request (for example, 240 free bytes versus a 180-byte request in Scenario A for First and Worst Fit). This is the definition of \textbf{external fragmentation}: enough memory exists, but not in a single contiguous hole. None of the three strategies eliminates it; they only change how it is distributed. This agrees with the literature, where simulations show that First Fit and Best Fit are generally better than Worst Fit in memory utilization, and First Fit is usually faster \cite{silberschatz}.

% ============================================================
\section{Observations on the base code}
% ============================================================

While analyzing and testing the simulator, the following points were identified. They belong to the base code, not to the strategies implemented in this deliverable, but they affect the behavior of any free memory strategy:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Merging of non-adjacent holes in \code{returnMemorySlot()}}. When the returned slot lies after the last free hole, the branch \code{list.getLast().addSlot(m)} adds its size to the last hole without checking that both are adjacent. For example, with a single free hole $[0, 100)$, returning the slot $[500, 550)$ produces the hole $[0, 150)$ instead of two separate holes. This was reproduced with the real class. A possible fix is to always insert the slot in order (\code{list.add(i+1, m)}) and let \code{fuseSlots()} decide whether it must be merged.
  \item \textbf{Aliasing of the returned slot}. The \code{MemorySlot} object of the finished process is inserted directly into the free list; when \code{fuseSlots()} later merges holes, it modifies that same object. As a consequence, at the end of Scenario 0 the memory manager of P0 reports a slot of size $1\,048\,576$. Inserting a copy (\code{new MemorySlot(m)}) would avoid this side effect.
  \item \textbf{Default configuration}. The repository keeps \code{MSM = FIRST\_FIT} in \code{OS.java}; to run the new strategies, this constant must be changed to \code{BEST\_FIT} or \code{WORST\_FIT}.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================
\section{General conclusions}
% ============================================================

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The implementations of \code{BestFitMemorySlotManager.java} and \code{WorstFitMemorySlotManager.java} select, in every tested case, exactly the hole predicted by their definitions, as confirmed by the match between the program output and the manual trace of the free-hole list in both fragmentation scenarios.
  \item The simulator's modular design made the extension straightforward: since the list management (splitting with \code{assignMemory}, reclamation and fusion) is inherited from \code{FreeMemorySlotManager}, each new strategy only had to implement the selection rule in \code{getSlot()}, and it is automatically used both for main memory and for swap memory.
  \item The integration test shows that the three strategies only differ when memory is already fragmented (more than one free hole at allocation time); with the default scenario of the simulator they produce identical results, so specific fragmentation scenarios are needed to evaluate them.
  \item The choice of strategy depends on the expected workload: Best Fit is preferable when request sizes vary widely and large holes must be preserved; Worst Fit when requests are of similar size; and First Fit when allocation speed matters most. In every case external fragmentation persists, which motivates non-contiguous techniques such as paging and segmentation.
\end{enumerate}

